% Engineering Software with Agentic AI -- Chapter 7 -- CP07 -- 19 October 2026.
% Original English editorial prose. Treasury identifiers, people, roles, data and examples are fictional.
% Synthetic Treasury examples; observed local process runs are distinguished from proposed plans.
% Prior-edition conceptual lineage documented in REUSE_REGISTER.md.
% Vendor docs rechecked as of 2026-10-20; deterministic workers are not live coding-client trials.
\chapter{Plan work, delegate tasks and coordinate responsibility}
\label{ch:plan-delegation}

An operator can now read a proposed explanation of a Treasury routing decision. For a valid amount below EUR~5,000, the service recommends the standard queue; at or above that amount, it recommends human review. The team has also explored two ways of producing that explanation. Before asking a coding agent to finish the feature, someone must decide which design to adopt.

That is where we resume the case study. The earlier chapters gave us a behavior specification, two prototypes and a packet of relevant sources. The direct adapter is the proposed design, but its architecture decision record, or \emph{ADR}, still awaits the technical owner's decision. The record is called \texttt{ADR-005}; the name helps us find it, while its status tells us what work may follow. The prototype's successful tests are useful input to that decision \parencite{papaliniNDSE2,papaliniNDLE,atlasV04B090}.

Suppose the next request is \enquote{Implement the chosen adapter and get the feature released.} Before dividing the work, read the word \emph{chosen} carefully. The source record says \emph{proposed}. We can investigate the design now, but the implementation task needs a decision that has not yet been made. Planning means making that dependency visible so that a fast worker does not run ahead of the team.

This chapter teaches how to delegate in that situation. We will first describe tasks in ordinary language, decide which can proceed together, and identify the person who may unblock the next step. We will then express the same plan as data. Only after those ideas are clear will the optional runtime exercises turn them into running processes. Earlier task-decomposition and coordination treatments provide the conceptual background \parencite{atlasV04B089,atlasV04B095}.

\begin{conceptbox}{A task graph connects work to its prerequisites}
A \emph{task} is a piece of work with a purpose and an observable result. A \emph{dependency} says that another result or decision is required first. A \emph{task graph} records these relationships. Two investigations can start together when neither needs the other's answer. An implementation must wait when it needs a design decision. The graph explains the order; assigning a worker does not remove a dependency.
\end{conceptbox}

\begin{readerbox}{What you will learn}
You will write a bounded task, distinguish a dependency from an approval, choose when parallel work is useful, and prepare a handoff that another person can use. The first exercise preserves an accurate pending plan. The continuing example then supplies explicit fictional owner decisions and validates the transition to implementation. You can also run a small orchestrator and examine an automatic harness-improvement experiment.
\end{readerbox}

\begin{readerbox}{Three learning routes}
\textbf{Core route.} Follow the planning sections in order, repair the two planning files in Section~\ref{sec:ch7-lab}, then read the accepted teaching continuation in Section~\ref{sec:ch7-accepted-continuation}. Complete the handoff and practice exercise with the distinction between the two plan versions clear.

\textbf{Controller extension.} Section~\ref{sec:ch7-orchestrator}, after the core exercise, shows how a program dispatches workers and handles failure. Its separate investigation graph gives concrete execution meaning to the planning concepts.

\textbf{Harness extension.} Section~\ref{sec:ch7-automatic-harness} shows how changing the workflow around a fixed executor changes its answers. Use a fresh, unchanged edition copy for that experiment. Its separate upstream demonstration requires Python~3.12 or~3.13. Both extensions use scripted workers, so you can study their mechanics without a model account. Neither extension resolves the pending architecture decision in the core plan.
\end{readerbox}

\section{Describe one delegated task}
\label{sec:ch7-task}

A broad request such as \enquote{finish the explanation feature} contains several different kinds of work. Someone must inspect the specified behavior, someone must examine the design trade-offs, and the technical owner must decide what may be implemented. A useful task separates one of these responsibilities and gives its worker a clear stopping point \parencite{papaliniNDSDD1,papaliniNDSDD2}.

Begin with the question the worker should answer. For the specification investigator, it is: \emph{Do the explanation examples preserve the routing rule and the separation from payment approval?} The worker reads the intent and specification, compares their statements, and returns a list of agreements or conflicts with source references. The result is an investigation that the owner can read. It does not require editing the implementation.

\begin{examplebox}{Build the first task card in six steps}
\textbf{Purpose:} check the explanation against the existing rule. \textbf{Sources:} identify the current intent and behavior examples. \textbf{Authority:} report a disagreement; leave policy decisions to the policy owner. \textbf{Write set:} allow no repository edits. \textbf{Dependencies:} none, because the source packet is already available. \textbf{Output:} return a source-linked inventory of the behavior cases and any conflicts.

These six answers explain why this task can start now. They also explain why \enquote{be the requirements expert} is an incomplete assignment: a role name says little about inputs, permitted actions or completion.
\end{examplebox}

The \emph{write set} is simply the list of paths a task may change. An \emph{authority boundary} identifies decisions the task may make and decisions it must leave to an owner. Keep them separate: permission to edit a source file does not give the worker permission to choose a new business rule.

The companion expresses these cards in \path{repo/engineering/planning/delegation_plan.json}. The supplied learner plan contains deliberate mistakes. The worked example in \path{reader_side/reference_solution/v2/delegation_plan.json} describes a permissible plan at the present stage. Read the table below as a translation of our reasoning into six named tasks; the numbers are labels, not a requirement for six agents.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering\footnotesize
\caption{Chapter 7's proposed tasks; the decision dependencies are not optional.}
\label{tab:ch7-task-list}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}p{0.13\textwidth}p{0.24\textwidth}X p{0.17\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Task} & \textbf{Actor / action} & \textbf{Dependency and evidence} & \textbf{Current state}\\
\midrule
T07-01 & Read-only specification investigator & Inspect approved intent and reference behavior examples; report conflicts. & Ready\\
T07-02 & Read-only architecture investigator & Inspect the two prototypes and ADR assumptions; report trade-offs. & Ready\\
T07-03 & Product and technical owners & Record separate decisions on SPEC-004 and ADR-005 after both investigations. & Human decisions pending\\
T07-04 & Bounded coding agent & Only after T07-03: propose one change in the approved adapter. & Blocked\\
T07-05 & Independent reviewer & Only after T07-04: check the candidate and owner-run test evidence. & Blocked\\
T07-06 & Product and release owners & Retain operator-copy review; after T07-05, decide release and rollback ownership. & Human decisions pending\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

The six tasks do not require six people. A person may combine investigation or coordination roles where the governing policy permits it. This reference plan nevertheless forbids the maker from approving their own work: the review must have an accountable reviewer other than the maker, and the architecture and release decisions retain their designated human owners. Conversely, using six virtual ``agents'' does not manufacture six independent perspectives. Tool separation, training similarities and shared inputs can correlate errors even when there are distinct labels \parencite{papaliniNDSE2,papaliniNDLE,atlasV04B095}.

\section{Include decisions in the dependency graph}
\label{sec:ch7-dependencies}

The diagram below shows the decision dependencies in the six-task teaching plan. Two investigations can begin with the reference specification and proposed design. Their results feed separate requirements and architecture decisions in T07-03. Both must be resolved before a revised plan can authorize a constrained edit. Independent review then precedes the operator-copy and release decisions retained in T07-06. Making those dependencies visible prevents a scheduler from treating missing authorization as an inconveniently slow software task.

The current v2 records carry the earlier requirements decision explicitly. The \texttt{pending\_reviews} collection retains \texttt{SPEC-004}, \texttt{ADR-005} and \texttt{OPERATOR\_COPY}, with their subjects, accountable roles and pending state. T07-03 names both \texttt{SPEC-004} and \texttt{ADR-005} in \texttt{decision\_subjects}; T07-06 names the operator-copy review alongside its later release responsibility. Grouping decisions in one task does not merge their authority. Requirements acceptance and design acceptance remain separate records.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=Latex,node distance=6mm, every node/.style={font=\scriptsize}, tstep/.style={draw=accent,rounded corners=2pt,align=center,minimum height=10mm,text width=0.31\linewidth,fill=pale}, gate/.style={draw=orange!70!black,rounded corners=2pt,align=center,minimum height=10mm,text width=0.31\linewidth,fill=orange!8}]
\node[tstep] (t1) {T07-01\\Read-only spec investigation};
\node[tstep,right=6mm of t1] (t2) {T07-02\\Read-only design investigation};
\coordinate (mid) at ($(t1.south)!0.5!(t2.south)$);
\node[gate,below=11mm of mid] (human) {T07-03\\Requirements and ADR decisions};
\node[tstep,below=8mm of human] (code) {T07-04\\One bounded writer};
\node[tstep,below=8mm of code] (review) {T07-05\\Independent review};
\node[gate,below=8mm of review] (release) {T07-06\\Copy and release decisions};
\draw[->,thick] (t1.south) |- (human.west);
\draw[->,thick] (t2.south) |- (human.east);
\draw[->,thick] (human) -- (code);
\draw[->,thick] (code) -- (review);
\draw[->,thick] (review) -- (release);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{A proposed dependency graph, not a recorded agent run. The orange human decisions prevent a green technical result from becoming unearned business or release authority.}
\label{fig:ch7-dependency-graph}
\end{figure}

Read an arrow as \enquote{the next task needs this result}. The two investigation arrows converge on the human decision task because the owners need both the behavior analysis and the design analysis. The implementation arrow leaves that task because the writer needs accepted requirements, a settled design and an explicitly revised plan. Review follows implementation because it must examine an actual candidate.

\begin{examplebox}{Walk the graph before running anything}
Suppose both investigations finish. First, retain their results. Second, give them to the technical owner. Third, inspect the owner's decision. Acceptance clears the architecture dependency. Before a revised plan can authorize implementation, the product and policy owners must also accept the proposed requirements. Rejection of the design sends us back to design work. Without an architecture decision, the writer remains blocked.

Now suppose the specification investigator finishes while the design investigator is still working. The finished task stays complete, but the architecture review lacks one required input. Starting the writer would skip the missing investigation and architecture decision, while the separate requirements review would still need to be resolved.
\end{examplebox}

For the implementation task, T07-03 collects the requirements and architecture decisions as distinct prerequisites, followed by an explicit update of the plan. A single green status cannot replace either owner's decision. Merely creating a file named \texttt{ADR-005.md}, or marking a discussion \enquote{done}, does not satisfy the architecture dependency. The baseline planning gate checks the pending state. Section~\ref{sec:ch7-accepted-continuation} then validates a distinct, explicitly fictional transition with both decisions present.

Three simple mistakes help us inspect a graph. A \emph{dangling dependency} points to a nonexistent task, so the prerequisite can never arrive. A \emph{cycle} occurs when A waits for B while B waits, directly or indirectly, for A. A \emph{false-ready task} appears executable only because the plan omitted a necessary prerequisite, such as the owner decision. The planning gate detects selected examples of these mistakes. Here, a \emph{gate} is a program that checks stated conditions and returns findings; it does not decide the business policy.

A running coordinator also needs rules for cancellation, retries, interrupted processes and changes to shared state. We will introduce those concerns in the optional controller exercise. The broader runtime treatment is developed in \emph{Non-Deterministic Loop Engineering} \parencite{papaliniNDLE}.

\section{Choose the smallest useful delegation topology}
\label{sec:ch7-topology}

A \emph{delegation topology} is the arrangement of workers and information flow: one worker, several investigators reporting to a coordinator, or a sequence in which one output becomes the next input. Start by identifying the missing information. The number of available agents is a later consideration.

Our case has two useful questions. The specification investigator asks whether the proposed explanation preserves the agreed behavior. The architecture investigator asks which trade-offs and unanswered questions the two prototypes leave. Each can inspect the existing sources without waiting for the other. Their findings can therefore be gathered in parallel, then read together by the owner. This pattern is often called \emph{fan out and join}: distribute independent questions, then combine their results.

Coding is different at this point. Both possible writers would need the same unresolved architecture decision and might edit the same implementation. Starting them together would add conflicting changes without supplying the missing decision. The reference plan consequently chooses \texttt{BOUNDED\_SINGLE\_WRITER}: two read-only investigations are possible now, and one narrowly scoped writer may be authorized later.

The original planning demonstration groups ready tasks into logical rounds. With one slot, it produces \texttt{[T07-01], [T07-02]}; with two slots, \texttt{[T07-01,T07-02]}. This tells us which work could overlap. It does not run the workers or tell us how much time a real team would save. Use the table and \path{practice/agent_workflow/pattern_selector.md} to choose a pattern from the task's dependencies.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering\small
\caption{Select a working pattern from the missing information and dependencies.}
\label{tab:ch7-agent-options}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}p{0.24\textwidth}X p{0.24\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{What is missing?} & \textbf{Initial pattern} & \textbf{Boundary to retain}\\
\midrule
One settled small edit & One attended writer and an owner-controlled check & Diff, intent and review decision\\
One known source & Search or a fixed read-only CLI/tool lookup & Source identity and authority\\
A recurring procedure & A narrow skill with explicit inputs/outputs & Applicability and current revision\\
Independent questions & Read-only delegates and one synthesizer & Conflicts and shared assumptions\\
Dependent writable work & Sequential plan; separate worktrees only where justified & Interface and merge ownership\\
An unresolved decision & Investigation only; keep implementation blocked & Actual owner decision and revised plan\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

This caution is supported by an important primary practitioner account. Anthropic's multi-agent research-system report describes substantial benefits for breadth-first research \emph{in its own evaluation}, but also notes much higher token use and says tasks with shared context and many dependencies are poor candidates for indiscriminate parallel agents. In particular, research exploration is not automatically interchangeable with concurrent coding inside the same mutable repository \parencite{anthropicMultiAgent2025}. We cite the author's evaluation and context, not an experimentally established universal multiplier for software engineering.

Use more elaborate patterns only when their extra decision is needed. A generator--critic pair makes a proposed change and its challenge explicit, but needs a separately controlled acceptance criterion. A router selects a specialist when task classes and routing failures can be checked. An advisor supplies bounded expertise while the coordinator retains the decision. Independent investigations can fan out and join when their required inputs and synthesis rule are explicit. The local controller below implements that last coordination shape; it does not implement every pattern or make its workers independent reviewers \parencite{papaliniNDLE,anthropicMultiAgent2025}.

Apply the selector to the current plan: the specification and design questions are ready, but their answers feed one unresolved architecture decision. Parallel investigation is optional; parallel coding would bypass that dependency. The next useful output is a source-linked uncertainty report, not more changed files. No selected pattern implies linear throughput or independent verification by default \parencite{atlasV04B095}.

\section{Give every delegated worker a readable, enforceable boundary}
\label{sec:ch7-boundaries}

A task boundary has an explanatory part and an enforcement part. The explanatory part tells the worker what to do. The enforcement part limits what its tools and execution account can actually do. If we describe an investigator as read-only but give it a shell that can rewrite the repository, our description has not removed that ability \parencite{papaliniNDLE,papaliniNDSDD2}.

For the two investigators, the proposed write set is empty. For the later implementation task, it contains one path: \path{src/treasury/architecture/direct.py}. The router, policy, expected test results, specification and architecture record belong outside that task's edits. This lets a reviewer ask a practical question: did the worker change only the file it was asked to change?

\begin{conceptbox}{Instructions, checks and restrictions have different jobs}
An instruction says \enquote{do not edit the expected results}. A comparison after the work can detect that the expected-results file changed. An access restriction can prevent that worker from writing the file in the first place. Use the words \emph{requested}, \emph{checked} and \emph{prevented} for these different observations.

The companion's owner-run scope guard and hashes demonstrate selected comparisons. They are not an operating-system sandbox. A real agent environment needs its own permission configuration when prevention is required.
\end{conceptbox}

Write the assignment so that another person can inspect it: \enquote{Read INT-001 and SPEC-004/v1, report mismatches, and leave policy and tests unchanged.} The identifiers locate the two source records already introduced. The expected output is a report of observations. Similarly, an independent reviewer needs expectations controlled separately from the proposed patch; a second agent reading only the maker's self-tests can inherit the same error \parencite{papaliniNDSE2}.

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Selected fields from the author-created CP07 implementation-task card; simplified from the reference JSON.}]
{
  "id": "T07-04",
  "kind": "CODING_AGENT",
  "depends_on": ["T07-03"],
  "write_paths": ["src/treasury/architecture/direct.py"],
  "state": "BLOCKED",
  "max_attempts": 1,
  "expected_output": "Inspectable diff and candidate revision"
}
\end{lstlisting}

The write path is a \emph{candidate target}, not an authorization to execute now. Until T07-03 is genuinely complete, the worker remains blocked. Once the technical owner accepts or revises the architecture, the plan would need a new version reflecting the actual decision and renewed source hashes. The reference JSON in this chapter intentionally models the \emph{pre-approval} state only.

\section{Budgets, retries and explicit escalation}
\label{sec:ch7-budgets}

A \emph{budget} limits the resources an assignment may consume. In a coding workflow those resources may include simultaneous workers, model calls, elapsed time and additional attempts. The limit provides a stopping rule: when the work cannot finish within it, the coordinator must return a useful account of the blockage rather than continuing indefinitely \parencite{papaliniNDLE,atlasV04B089}.

The teaching plan allows at most two concurrent agents, six model invocations, one retry per task and 45 minutes elapsed time. These are invented exercise ceilings. Each reference task has the stricter setting \texttt{max\_attempts=1}, so it gets one execution and no automatic retry. The global budget does not enlarge a task's own allowance.

\begin{examplebox}{Count attempts before granting a retry}
An \emph{attempt} is one execution. A \emph{retry} is another execution after an earlier attempt. If a task permits two attempts, the first failure leaves at most one retry. If it permits one attempt, the first failure exhausts its allowance.

Now apply this to the architecture decision. Repeating a model call cannot provide the missing owner's acceptance. The correct next step is to request that decision, regardless of unused process slots or model-call budget.
\end{examplebox}

Use the failure to choose the next action. A failed source lookup may justify a narrower query. An out-of-scope write calls for stopping and inspecting the boundary. A changed source may invalidate an earlier result, meaning that the result no longer supports a claim about the current inputs. Chapter~6 explained how source dependencies identify which conclusions need reconsideration.

A worker's confidence is useful context, but completion needs an observable result: a test report for the candidate, a reviewed artifact or the owner's decision. The JSON plan records the intended limits; the execution environment must enforce the applicable limits. The optional controller exercise below will make a small subset of that enforcement visible \parencite{papaliniNDSE2,papaliniNDLE}.

\section{What current coding-agent products provide}
\label{sec:ch7-products}

A portable task contract is useful because coding-agent products expose different delegation mechanisms. The following descriptions reflect official documentation rechecked on 20 October 2026, rather than a live client trial. A YAML profile, a cloud worker, a separate context window, and an isolated writable worktree have different semantics.

\paragraph{GitHub Copilot.} GitHub's current documentation describes custom agents whose repository-level profiles are Markdown files under \path{.github/agents/}. Profiles can specify prompts and available tools. The precise set of fields and support varies by Copilot surface. One particularly important detail applies to Copilot CLI: when a \emph{custom} agent is spawned as a subagent, it does not automatically inherit the repository's instruction files; \texttt{include-custom-instructions: true} opts it in. Some built-in agents have different defaults. This is why ``we have an AGENTS.md'' is not a complete delegation control \parencite{githubCopilotCustom2026,githubCopilotSubagent2026}.

\paragraph{Claude Code.} Project subagent definitions live under \path{.claude/agents/}. Its documentation distinguishes a subagent's context, tool access and permission modes. It also describes agent teams as an experimental feature, disabled by default in the documentation checked for this review. A read-only research subagent is appropriate when exploration would otherwise flood the primary context. A team of writable sessions requires coordination over shared files, state and changes. A file in \path{.claude/agents/} is still configuration, not itself proof that repository privileges are safely constrained \parencite{claudeSubagents2026,claudeTeams2026}.

\paragraph{Codex and OpenAI agent runtimes.} OpenAI's Agents API documentation supports bounded multi-agent delegation for independent questions and warns that dependent steps should often remain with the coordinator. In that API, a subagent's separate context does \emph{not} imply a separate filesystem: subagents can share an execution environment. The statement is runtime-specific, not a claim that every Codex interface exposes the same controls. Use the documented controls of the particular runtime selected for the exercise \parencite{openaiMultiAgent2026,openaiAgents2026}.

\paragraph{Other environments.} Cursor and Kiro offer their own conventions for project guidance, specs and automation. Chapter~2 introduced how to discover and configure those surfaces. Here we keep the task graph in portable JSON and document an illustrative translation guide for each client. No agent-hosting tool is claimed to have consumed the CP07 plan or enforced its write sets during the offline test. When the publisher's review environment is available, the guide should be pinned to a client version and validated end to end.

A Git worktree can be useful if two authorized agents must work on independent writable branches. The \texttt{git worktree} command creates multiple working trees for one repository, each with a separate checked-out working tree and index. Those worktrees can nevertheless share repository-level resources and still require merge, review, policy and conflict handling. They are not an automatic process sandbox or independent release-approval mechanism \parencite{gitWorktreeDoc2026}.

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Illustrative Git commands; do not run these on an uncommitted working tree without reviewing its state.}]
# In a suitable temporary, initialized Git repository:
git status --short
git worktree add -b ch07-readonly ../ch07-readonly HEAD
git worktree list
# Inspect the resulting worktree and remove it when no longer needed.
\end{lstlisting}

For our reference plan, launching writable parallel worktrees would add complexity without resolving the missing architecture decision. \emph{Do less first} is an engineering choice, not a failure to exploit agentic technology.

\section{Repair the planning files}
\input{entrycards/chapter07.tex}


\label{sec:ch7-lab}

The core lab asks you to repair a plan, not to implement the feature. You will edit two JSON files: the task plan and its handoff. The supplied version deliberately overstates what the team has decided. Your job is to make it agree with the source packet and with the dependency reasoning developed above.

The inputs are the intent, the reference behavior specification, the proposed design record and the context packet from Chapter~6. The gate compares their recorded SHA-256 values. A \emph{digest} is a fingerprint computed from a file's bytes: matching the saved fingerprint establishes that this is the saved version. It does not establish who approved the content. We need the source's meaning and status as well as its identity.

Run the two commands below before editing. The first checks the supplied teaching infrastructure. The second checks your current planning files and initially reports failures. Keep that first result and explain one finding in your own words before attempting a repair. The worked-example comparison comes after your attempt and handoff.

\Needspace{10\baselineskip}
\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Run the owner-controlled CP07 checks from the companion directory.}]
python3 reader_side/tools/run_checks.py
python3 reader_side/tools/planning_gate.py
# The starter plan is intentionally unsafe: expected FAIL.
\end{lstlisting}

Use \texttt{python3 reader\_side/tools/run\_checks.py} for the current shared result and \texttt{planning\_gate.py} for the plan you selected. Expect the unsafe starter to fail and an accurately pending plan to pass its planning checks. That pass means the dependencies and boundaries are represented correctly; it does not execute an agent or supply the missing owner decisions.

The starter's failures are instructive. It changes the policy threshold to EUR~3,000, treats payment approval as permitted, declares ADR-005 accepted and release approved, assigns writes to a supposedly read-only investigator, allows alteration of a protected acceptance test, removes the coding task's human dependency, and fabricates execution evidence. In a realistic system these errors would arise through multiple paths, not necessarily in one JSON file. The teaching fixture combines them so learners can distinguish \emph{authority}, \emph{sequence}, \emph{file scope}, \emph{evidence} and \emph{resource budget} failures.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering\small
\caption{Selected negative findings and the engineering decision each forces.}
\label{tab:ch7-gate}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}p{0.27\textwidth}X@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Finding family} & \textbf{Necessary correction}\\
\midrule
\texttt{AUTHORITY} & Restore current policy and human-only design/release decisions.\\
\texttt{DEPENDENCIES} / \texttt{STATE} & Block the writer until the required owners act; do not invent completed review.\\
\texttt{READ\_ONLY\_WRITES} & Remove writable paths from investigator and reviewer cards.\\
\texttt{PROTECTED\_PATH} & Keep the oracle, specification and ADR outside the coding agent's allowed output.\\
\texttt{HANDOFF} & Report pending decisions and no execution evidence; do not claim delivery.\\
\texttt{BUDGET} / \texttt{PARALLEL} & Select a bounded, justified degree of concurrency.\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

Work through one finding before correcting the rest. If T07-04 has no dependency on T07-03, the writer appears ready. Restore that dependency, keep the owner's decision pending, and mark the writer blocked. Then check the handoff: it must describe the same pending decision and blocked writer. Correcting only the task card would leave the next worker with a contradictory summary.

\begin{practicebox}{Carry the repaired starter into the current schema}
Repairing the old false approvals is only the first step. The cumulative route also needs the complete review register introduced in Chapter~6.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Set the plan's schema to \texttt{delegation-plan/v2} and the handoff's to \texttt{delegation-handoff/v2}. Set \texttt{record\_revision} to 2 in both. Keep the business identity \texttt{PLAN-007/v1}; a record revision does not approve that plan.
\item Carry all three \texttt{pending\_reviews} records from the current context packet into both files. Preserve each subject, owner role, \texttt{PENDING\_HUMAN} status and null decision reference.
\item Set T07-03's \texttt{decision\_subjects} to \texttt{["SPEC-004", "ADR-005"]}; set T07-06's to \texttt{["OPERATOR\_COPY"]}. Its release responsibility remains separately stated in the task.
\item Update the CTX-006 source receipt to \path{reader_side/reference_solution/v2/context_packet.json} and calculate SHA-256 from that file's actual bytes. Preserve the other pinned historical source receipts.
\item Run the planning gate on your two files. Once the earlier candidates are repaired, run the cumulative gate too; a locally valid legacy plan cannot silently drop SPEC-004 at the chapter boundary.
\end{enumerate}
The field-by-field guide is \path{reader_side/schemas/REVIEW_SCHEMAS_V2.md}. Compare your edits with the worked pair only after explaining why each pending decision must survive the handoff.
\end{practicebox}

\subsection*{Compare the worked example after your attempt}

Retain your edited pair, the gate output and a handoff explaining any unresolved decisions before opening the worked example. Then run the following command. It selects the protected reference pair without overwriting your candidates. In a reader observation session, record that this comparison is new solution exposure.

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Inspect a permissible pending plan after retaining your attempt.}]
python3 reader_side/tools/planning_gate.py \
  --plan reader_side/reference_solution/v2/delegation_plan.json \
  --handoff reader_side/reference_solution/v2/delegation_handoff.json
# The reference plan is a permissible *pending* state: expected PASS.
\end{lstlisting}

The reference ends with T07-03 \texttt{PENDING\_HUMAN}, T07-04 and T07-05 \texttt{BLOCKED}, and a separate release decision still required for T07-06. The planning check can pass in this state because it checks whether the plan represents the situation accurately. It does not require every task to be complete.

\section{Continue after the owners decide}\label{sec:ch7-accepted-continuation}

An accurate blocked plan is a starting point. To finish the story, open \path{practice/lifecycle/teaching_plan.json}. This is \texttt{PLAN-007/v2-TEACHING}, a new fictional continuation that preserves v1 as the earlier negative example. A fictional product and policy owner accepts the behavior contract; a fictional technical owner accepts the direct design. Both decisions identify the same contract and candidate. A design decision alone cannot settle the pending requirements question.

The revised task also corrects its scope. The direct adapter already exists, so the next writer repairs the actual Chapter~8 wrapper and adds a plain-language operator view. The new plan explicitly supersedes the old proposed edit to \path{direct.py}; it never reports that unused edit as completed. The validator checks the decisions before writing and compares the resulting candidate bytes afterwards. Changing a status to \texttt{APPROVED}, omitting the requirements decision or substituting another candidate leaves the transition rejected.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=bash,caption={Run the continuing worked example in a new directory.}]
python3 practice/lifecycle/run_lifecycle.py \
  --output ../lifecycle_run_01
\end{lstlisting}

This command creates an isolated copy, records checkpoints, and follows the same change through implementation, independent selected-case review, local release, policy history and incident recovery. Begin with \path{evidence/TECHNICAL_TRANSITION.json}; later chapters explain its subsequent outputs. The decisions are author-supplied fiction for this local exercise. Their purpose is to make a valid transition observable without inventing real human acceptance or production authority.

\section{Prepare a handoff another worker can use}
\label{sec:ch7-handoff}

A \emph{handoff} is the short record a new worker needs to continue without reconstructing the whole conversation. In this case it should answer four questions: what sources were inspected, what has actually been done, what can happen next, and what is still waiting for a decision? The handoff file records the source IDs, ready and blocked tasks, pending human decisions and execution results.

Compare two possible summaries. \enquote{The direct adapter is ready; implement it} drops the pending decision. \enquote{Two investigations are ready; the direct adapter remains proposed and implementation awaits requirements and architecture decisions} preserves the next action. Because this chapter prepares a plan rather than executing those investigations, the reference's execution-evidence list is empty. That empty list is useful information.

A handoff must also explain what would change the plan. If the owner rejects the direct adapter, the writer's proposed path and task need revision. If the policy owner changes the routing rule, the relevant intent and specification need reassessment. Record those conditions so that a shorter context or a fresh agent session does not turn an old proposal into current authority \parencite{atlasV04B090,papaliniNDSE2}.

\begin{warningbox}{What this gate does not prove}
The Python guard validates selected fields, fingerprints and relationships in a synthetic fixture. It does not inspect the internal intentions of a model, create OS-enforced sandboxes, intercept live Copilot/Claude/Codex tool calls, prevent an attacker from changing an environment they control, or exercise a real multi-agent runtime. It also does not replace independent acceptance testing or human decisions. Every such gate must be supplied and tested separately before any production use.
\end{warningbox}

\section{Practice exercise: design the smallest accountable plan}
\label{sec:ch7-exercise}

Begin by reading the four source receipts. Explain which source represents authorized policy, which represents reference behavior, which represents a proposed design, and which is a context selection rather than an approval. Open the deliberately wrong plan and handoff, then run the planning gate. Do not change the underlying routing engine, protected tests, source catalogue, or the human architecture decision. Repair only the two named JSON files under \path{repo/engineering/planning/}. Re-run both the gate and cumulative suite. Preserve the original failure output as evidence of what changed.

Then answer three design questions in a short review note. First, are two read-only investigations genuinely independent, and what common assumptions might bias both? Second, would parallel writable worktrees make this task faster, or merely add coordination before the technical owner has acted? Third, what evidence would justify unblocking T07-04, and whose authority is required? Include a reversal trigger: name one change to architecture or policy that would invalidate the current plan. A successful submission is not one in which every task is marked \texttt{DONE}. It is one in which the graph accurately expresses the current state and its next authorized decision.

\begin{practicebox}{Check your reasoning before consulting the reference}
\textbf{Question:} both investigations have finished, but the owner has not decided. May T07-04 start? \textbf{Answer:} no. Completed investigations provide evidence for a decision; they do not make it. The writer needs both recorded requirements acceptance and architecture acceptance, followed by an updated plan. T07-03's decision subjects and the complete pending review register preserve those distinct prerequisites.

\textbf{Question:} can the planning gate pass with a blocked writer? \textbf{Answer:} yes. Its purpose is to check an accurate, permissible plan. The blocked state correctly represents the missing decision.

\textbf{Transfer:} imagine that the owner rejects the direct adapter. Identify the task, write path and handoff statements you would reconsider before assigning any writer. Explain the dependency in your own words before editing the JSON.
\end{practicebox}

\begin{readerbox}{Reviewer rubric}
\textbf{Contract fidelity:} The threshold and no-payment boundary are preserved. \textbf{Authority:} The structured review register preserves requirements, architecture and operator-copy decisions with their named owners; task dependencies retain the later release decision. \textbf{Graph:} Dependencies are acyclic, explicit and coherent with task states. \textbf{Write scope:} Investigators and reviewers are read-only; the candidate writer has one named path and is blocked. \textbf{Evidence:} Input digests and the absence of real execution are declared. \textbf{Economics and simplicity:} Concurrency is justified by work independence rather than an unsupported claim of speed. \textbf{Transfer:} The student can explain how the plan would change if a human rejected the proposed architecture.
\end{readerbox}

