\section{What this chapter establishes}
\label{sec:ch7-conclusion}

The central contribution of agentic delegation to software engineering is not another role name. It is the possibility of distributing bounded work while keeping decisions, evidence and state inspectable. That opportunity creates new responsibilities: specify tasks precisely, partition writes, control budgets, protect the verifier, record handoffs and ensure people with the appropriate authority actually decide what has been accepted.

Two independent investigations can be \emph{planned} in parallel while implementation remains blocked; the owner-run gate detects selected false-ready and false-approval errors. This does not show that two agents outperform one. The new teaching continuation then makes the missing decisions explicit, validates their applicability and revises the implementation scope. Chapter~8 follows that same accepted fictional change through the actual wrapper repair and useful operator output. The old blocked plan remains available to show why the transition could not occur merely by changing a status label.

The added runtime exercises make another boundary visible: an orchestrator actually dispatches and reconciles work, while automatic harness engineering changes the execution recipe and tests the result. Their deterministic workers expose dependencies, failures and composition decisions without attributing the observations to an untested model. The same evidence discipline must survive when a real agent adapter replaces a scripted worker.
